  "notes": "FILE ORDERING / CHRONOLOGY (per task's non-obvious-order note): confirmed by content, not filenames. \"PPL_Loan_Restuctured.xlsx\" (no date suffix) is the ORIGINAL/baseline schedule -- it matches EX_DATA['PPL'] almost exactly (same 4 disbursements of 90,000,000 / 8,715,714.29 / 39,593,464.29 / 379,545,656.25 on 42808/42808/43143/43380, same 10%->9.25%->11% rate path, Level Principal, 360-day basis, 20 rate-bearing principal installments) and was therefore NOT emitted as a version -- it is the reference original, not a reschedule event. The three dated files were processed in this chronological order, confirmed by (a) matching opening/closing balances at each boundary and (b) an explicit cell comment in the \"2nd Restuctured_Dec'21.xlsx\" workbook itself reading \"to be assigned to Dec'22 Principal after 1st rescheduling\", which only makes sense if the 2nd Restructure (Dec'21) was booked BEFORE the 1st Rescheduling (Oct'22) despite the ordinal numbers: (1) PPL_Loan_2nd Restuctured_Dec'21.xlsx -- diverges from the original at 2021-03-31 (both had closing=683,967,232.74 at 2020-12-31); extends the interest-only moratorium so principal resumption (previously due 2021-03-31 per original) is pushed to 2022-03-31. Classified MORATORIUM_EXTENSION since only the deferral changed, not the rate. (2) PPL_Loan_1st Rescheduled_Oct'22.xlsx -- identical to the 2nd Restructure file through 2022-03-31 (verified row-by-row), diverges at 2022-06-30: principal is deferred for two more quarters (Jun'22, Sep'22 both zero-principal, whereas the 2nd Restructure file had continued paying 26.3M/qtr), then resumes 2022-12-29 at a recalculated higher amount (29,909,771.64/qtr vs 26,306,432.03/qtr). Classified RESTRUCTURE (re-amortization + repeated deferral, not just a rate change). A cell comment \"Rescheduled Amount\" sits on the 2022-09-29->2022-12-29 closing-balance cell in this file, corroborating this as the reschedule point. (3) PPL_Loan_4th Rescheduled_Dec'24.xlsx -- identical to the 1st Reschedule file through the opening/interest/principal of the 2024-09-30 installment (opening 478,556,346.23, interest 11,006,795.96, principal 29,909,771.64 match exactly), but the CLOSING balance for that same 2024-09-30 installment jumps to 550,075,083.17 instead of the previously-projected 448,646,574.59 -- an overdue roll-in of 101,428,508.57 within that single row (this workbook's own Summary sheet shows a \"Repayment overdue\" (principal shortfall) of 71,518,736.93 around Aug/Sep 2024 plus additional accrued/overdue interest and possibly LPC not separately broken out; the exact split between overdue principal, overdue interest, and LPC could not be cleanly decomposed from the workbook and is reported as a single overdueAmountRolledIn figure). A new rate change to 12% (from 9%) effective 2024-12-30 is also introduced in this same file (confirmed via implied-rate back-calculation from interest/opening-balance/day-count on both sides of that date: 9.00% for the period ending 2024-12-30, 12.00% for the period ending 2025-03-31 onward). Classified as RESTRUCTURE (overdue capitalization + rate change together, per instructions a restructure that also changes rate stays RESTRUCTURE). Loan fully amortizes to 0 by 2030-03-30 under this version -- no further reschedule file exists for PPL, so this is presumably the currently governing schedule as of 2026-07-07. ADDITIONAL OBSERVATION: all three post-original workbooks (and even the \"PPL_Loan_Restuctured.xlsx\" baseline itself) contain a THIRD historical rate change not present in EX_DATA['PPL'] -- a rate reduction to 9% effective 2020-10-01 (i.e. EX_DATA['PPL'].interest_rate_change_events = [[42948,0.0925],[43191,0.11]] appears to be missing a [[44105-ish serial for 2020-10-01, 0.09]] entry that is present identically in every workbook examined here, including the presumed-original one). This is outside my assigned scope (I was told to trust the original EX_DATA fixture as already validated) but is flagged here since it could mean the \"original\" fixture in examples.ts is itself missing a rate-reduction event that predates all three reschedules I processed -- every one of my three versions' \"interest_rate\" baseline (0.09) already reflects this pre-existing change, which I carried forward as an ambient fact rather than a new event tied to any of my three files. All amounts are BDT. Used override arrays (opening_balance_amounts / interest_payment_amounts / closing_balance_amounts) for all three versions rather than first-principles regeneration, because each version has irregular stub periods (e.g. 91/92/93-day quarters with actual/360 day-count), a rate change mid-schedule (version 3), and a non-formulaic overdue roll-in (version 3) that cannot be cleanly reproduced from grace/tenor/rate parameters alone -- this mirrors the MCML/QPSL/SCBL/SKS/THERMAX override pattern already used in examples.ts. waivedAmount is 0 for all three events -- no evidence of forgiveness (as opposed to capitalization/deferral) was found in any workbook, comments, or Summary sheets. No explicit DFIM circular / Bangladesh Bank approval reference was found in any of the three workbooks' cells or comments (the only regulatory-sounding comment found, \"IDCOL Board in its 222nd meeting held on 29 Dec'16 approved rate reduction to 10%\", refers to the original loan's initial rate setting, not to any of the three reschedule events), so regulatoryReference is null for all three."
